    "q-342771": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342771",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /3}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $f(t)=cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /3)s}\\cdot L\\{cos\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
