    "q-342811": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342811",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-5(t-3)}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{(s-4)(s+5)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-4)(s+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-4)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s-4}+\\frac{B}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+5)+B(s-4).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(9)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-9)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{1}{s-4}-\\frac{1}{s+5}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{9}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+5}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{-5t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
