    "q-342810": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342810",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-4(t-2)}\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s+3)(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{B}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+4)+B(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(1)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)[{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
